WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:08.460
What’s up Wisecrack, Helen here! It’s
been a minute, but Jared’s giving me the

00:00:08.470 --> 00:00:12.950
mic today to get brainy with you about Big
Mouth, the Netflix series that puts a spotlight

00:00:12.950 --> 00:00:15.890
on everyone’s most discomforting season
of life: Puberty.

00:00:15.890 --> 00:00:21.060
A few of you have asked us to cover Big Mouth
but coming up with a good angle admittedly

00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:26.210
hasn’t been easy. On the surface, Big Mouth
isn’t the most profound thing in the world.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:29.500
I mean, it’s basically what you’d get
if the Simpsons and Degrassi gave birth to

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:33.980
a weird sex ed class that’s set to the tune
of catchy parody songs about menstrual blood

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:36.100
and “doing sex” on a woman.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:39.900
“Hey Rick, it’s not weird that I haven’t,
y’know, done sex yet, right?”

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:44.420
But we saw a couple possible angles from a
neuroscience and psychology standpoint that

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:48.829
maybe sorta kinda work, which is why I’m
here to walk you through ‘em. So, grab your

00:00:48.829 --> 00:00:54.450
bag of dicks and hold on tight for this half-baked
breakdown of Big Mouth. And yes, spoilers

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:55.450
ahead.

00:00:55.450 --> 00:00:57.810
But before we get to that, wanna let you guys
know this video is sponsored by Betterhelp,

00:00:57.810 --> 00:01:02.149
the online counseling service. You might be
aware that there was some controversy surrounding

00:01:02.149 --> 00:01:06.490
the company. We’re aware too and have addressed
these concerns in our Philosophy of True Detective

00:01:06.490 --> 00:01:09.960
video. The link’s in the description, if
you want to check it out. Long story short,

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:14.150
we all believe in the value of speaking to
someone on a regular basis and we think Betterhelp

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:18.100
is a good option for people who think online
therapy is the right fit for them. So, stick

00:01:18.100 --> 00:01:21.230
around till the end of the video for more
info, and now back to the show.

00:01:21.230 --> 00:01:24.909
Ok, so, for those of you who haven’t seen
the show, here’s a quick rundown.

00:01:24.909 --> 00:01:29.340
Big Mouth covers all your standard puberty
plots. In season 1, our heroes have their

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:33.840
first kisses, wrangle with their sexuality,
knock up a bean-filled pillow - you know,

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.770
typical teenager stuff. Most of this is on
account of the Hormone Monsters, big hairy

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:44.320
creatures who are constantly telling our heroes
how to live their lives and, more importantly,

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:54.880
where to put their genitals [“There we go,
visualize, focus, interesting, yeeeeess”].

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.649
In season 2, they’re basically doing the
same things, but with a new shoulder devil

00:01:58.649 --> 00:02:00.950
thrown in the mix: The Shame Wizard.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:05.990
All these youthful foibles gave us our first
pitch: The crazy pubescent brain.

00:02:05.990 --> 00:02:10.450
Scientists find Human adolescents fascinating
for a reason: Their rapidly-changing brains

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:15.939
make them like no other creature on Earth.
In the words of neuroscientist Frances Jensen,

00:02:15.939 --> 00:02:19.900
teenagers are “like Ferraris with weak brakes,”
which is why they’re more prone than adults

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:23.989
are to doing erratic shit, like giving in
to the thrill of shoplifting or running away

00:02:23.989 --> 00:02:31.049
with your not-boyfriend and his pillow-slash-baby
mama [Pam? Scorpion? Jay? Who are these pillows?

00:02:31.049 --> 00:02:35.569
We’re his family. Who the F*ck are you b*tch?].
That’s because the part of the brain that’s

00:02:35.569 --> 00:02:38.989
responsible for what we call “executive
functioning” - which includes things like

00:02:38.989 --> 00:02:43.719
making rational decisions - isn’t all the
way developed yet. All of this madness kicks

00:02:43.719 --> 00:02:47.889
off during puberty, when the brain begins
to undergo a whole lotta changes that will

00:02:47.889 --> 00:02:52.769
eventually turn you into a grown-ass human
being - maybe [Oh, Hey Nick! Coach Steve?

00:02:52.769 --> 00:02:57.830
How ‘bout an awkward reaching fist bump
my dude? What are you doing here? Oh. I still

00:02:57.830 --> 00:03:01.043
go to the pediatrician. I am what is known
in the medical community as a man-baby. You’re

00:03:01.043 --> 00:03:02.719
the man-baby Steve.].

00:03:02.719 --> 00:03:09.980
And you might be saying, no shit. While scientific
explanations of something we all mostly know

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:14.739
is a worthwhile endeavor, it’s not exactly
a Wisecrack video. So, I’m gonna give that

00:03:14.739 --> 00:03:16.669
pitch like a C-.

00:03:16.669 --> 00:03:18.969
That leaves us with our second almost-angle:
Shame.

00:03:18.969 --> 00:03:24.459
There was a bit of buzz around the Shame Wizard
when Season 2 debuted last fall, because A)

00:03:24.459 --> 00:03:29.049
David Thewlis, AKA Professor Lupin from Harry
Potter, voices the Shame Wizard and does a

00:03:29.049 --> 00:03:35.180
kick-ass job, and B) the character is just
so good and something all of us have dealt

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:36.180
with.

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:40.590
But apart from throwing a wet blanket on the
Hormone Monsters’ schemes, does the Shame

00:03:40.590 --> 00:03:45.439
Wizard actually reflect our lived experience
of shame in a unique and thought-provoking

00:03:45.439 --> 00:03:46.439
manner?

00:03:46.439 --> 00:03:51.669
Well, what IS shame, anyway? It may be best
described by contrasting it with guilt. Now,

00:03:51.669 --> 00:03:56.279
guilt is feeling bad about something that
we’ve done, while shame is feeling like

00:03:56.279 --> 00:04:01.919
we’re fundamentally flawed as the people
we are. Or, as the Shame Wizard puts it during

00:04:01.919 --> 00:04:07.540
Andrew’s trial: [“Now the state shall
prove that Andrew Glouberman didn’t do a

00:04:07.540 --> 00:04:12.109
bad thing. Yeah. He is a bad thing! Oh.”,]

00:04:12.109 --> 00:04:18.359
So, along with all the warm fuzzy feelings
of first love or lust or whatever, puberty

00:04:18.359 --> 00:04:23.680
also brings an awful lot of shameful self-loathing.
It’s a common refrain that the older you

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:29.930
get, the fewer fucks you give. And in that
vein, Big Mouth asks: How does our shame change

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:35.800
as we age? [“You’re just a thirteen-year-old
man who took what he wanted. Ah. Like me,

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:40.040
I live balls out and I give zero f*cks”]

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:43.979
The Shame Wizard shows up as a little voice
in Andrew’s head after he gets caught jerking

00:04:43.979 --> 00:04:50.690
off over Leah’s swimsuit [It’s going to
be fine. No, it’s not. What? She saw everything.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:56.710
Who said that? She’s going to tell everyone.”].
He proceeds to follow the kids around berating

00:04:56.710 --> 00:05:01.039
them for the things they do, much to the chagrin
of the hormone monsters [“Just give the

00:05:01.039 --> 00:05:05.740
kid a night off. Give all the kids a night
off.”]. But as far as the show tells us,

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:10.110
the Shame Wizard doesn’t have any power
over adults. Despite the fact that their behavior

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:14.550
is way more messed up, like Jessi’s mom
cheating on her dad and then banishing him

00:05:14.550 --> 00:05:20.229
to the basement and Jay’s parents generally
being sleezy assholes [“I will happily abandon

00:05:20.229 --> 00:05:25.080
my family to get you the divorce settlement
that you deserve.”]. I mean the poor guy’s

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:28.949
mom gets drunk and forgets to pick him up
from school on a daily basis, then sleeps

00:05:28.949 --> 00:05:32.849
with his teacher, all while his dad is out
banging other women and getting criminals

00:05:32.849 --> 00:05:36.759
off the hook. And these adults all seem to
avoid shame court.

00:05:36.759 --> 00:05:40.729
This gets really interesting in the context
of the relationship between the Shame Wizard

00:05:40.729 --> 00:05:45.889
and clinically-certified man child Coach Steve.
He’s the only adult that we see interacting

00:05:45.889 --> 00:05:49.560
with Shame, and he’s apparently immune to
it, even though he sleeps with the mother

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:53.970
of his student and so-called best friend and
knocks out a fellow teacher with a bat. You

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:58.599
might say the Shame Wizard can’t get to
Coach Steve because he’s just plain dumb.

00:05:58.599 --> 00:06:03.349
But even if that’s the case, it still doesn’t
change the fact that the teens are the only

00:06:03.349 --> 00:06:05.900
ones who are visibly tormented by the Shame
Wizard.

00:06:05.900 --> 00:06:10.949
But for the sake of science, was 13-year-old
you just being melodramatic when you felt

00:06:10.949 --> 00:06:14.370
like holing yourself up for the rest of your
life after you called your English teacher

00:06:14.370 --> 00:06:17.789
Mom? Or was your brain actually conspiring
against you?

00:06:17.789 --> 00:06:21.870
While admittedly there’s not a ton of clear-cut
evidence that teenagers are more shame-ridden

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:27.490
than adults, there is some. Susceptibility
to shame decreased as kids entered young adulthood

00:06:27.490 --> 00:06:31.780
in one study, something the researchers thought
might have to do with the fact that as your

00:06:31.780 --> 00:06:36.319
brain matures, it’s better able to regulate
emotions. And this makes sense when you think

00:06:36.319 --> 00:06:40.669
about how teens process emotions compared
to adults. Teens are known to pick up on social

00:06:40.669 --> 00:06:45.129
and emotional cues that adults just filter
out. They also spend more time thinking about

00:06:45.129 --> 00:06:49.699
the way others see them, traits that psychologists
think are from an evolutionary standpoint

00:06:49.699 --> 00:06:54.710
supposed to help them make friends. This has
the unfortunate side effect of making them

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:59.999
a lot more easily embarrassed and maybe even
ashamed. In a kind of creepy study where people

00:06:59.999 --> 00:07:04.409
were told that they were being monitored over
video, teens were more likely to report feeling

00:07:04.409 --> 00:07:08.840
embarrassed than either adults or children.
And parts of the brain associated with social

00:07:08.840 --> 00:07:12.050
emotions were a lot more active in teens during
this scenario.

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:16.289
Some scientists think this might have to do
with the influx of hormones during puberty,

00:07:16.289 --> 00:07:21.620
aka the hormone monsters showing up. So theoretically,
being rejected as a teenager is harder to

00:07:21.620 --> 00:07:26.250
swallow than when you’re an adult because
your brain is more sensitive to social emotions,

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:28.470
making the whole experience a lot more painful.

00:07:28.470 --> 00:07:33.529
Big Mouth also does a really great job of
highlighting the relationship between shame

00:07:33.529 --> 00:07:38.420
and depression. The Shame Wizard antagonizes
pretty much all the kids at some point, but

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:43.130
none of them are hit quite as hard as Jessi.
All she wants is for her family to be together,

00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:47.970
but she winds up responsible for her dad getting
kicked out of the house after she steals his

00:07:47.970 --> 00:07:53.900
weed gummies. The Shame Wizard bears down
on her hard [“Was it worth it Jessi? Just

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:59.610
to get hella faded” “No”], only to return
in full force after she slut shames Gina.

00:07:59.610 --> 00:08:03.759
Gina refuses to forgive her and calls her
a hypocrite, ultimately leading to Connie

00:08:03.759 --> 00:08:08.069
being replaced by the Depression Kitty and
Jessi being locked away in a padded room in

00:08:08.069 --> 00:08:13.210
the Department of Puberty [“I should get
up, it’s kind of weird to be in bed in the

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:14.849
middle of the day” “No it’s not”].

00:08:14.849 --> 00:08:18.510
Both guilt and shame are linked to depression,
but it’s been argued that shame might be

00:08:18.510 --> 00:08:22.189
more potent in actually causing depression
than guilt.

00:08:22.189 --> 00:08:26.449
That idea kind of offers an explanation for
why Jessi is the only major character who

00:08:26.449 --> 00:08:31.189
winds up depressed, despite the fact that
shame impacts everyone. Obviously, her home

00:08:31.189 --> 00:08:35.690
life is significantly more messed up than
anyone else’s, except maybe for Jay. But

00:08:35.690 --> 00:08:40.159
I’d argue that Jessi’s shame runs a lot
deeper than that of her friends, because the

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:45.741
consequences of her actions reflect more negatively
on her character than her friends’ do. Even

00:08:45.741 --> 00:08:50.190
though Andrew feels shame for being a quote
“pervert,” his masturbation habits can

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:53.920
easily be adjusted [“Actually I don’t
think I am going to jerk off inches from my

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:57.800
best friend’s face tonight. I am better
than that”]. But Jessi can’t go back and

00:08:57.800 --> 00:09:02.690
fix the things that lead her to believe she’s
a bad daughter and a hypocrite. That combined

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:07.190
with the fact that her family’s support
is kind of lacking, makes her more prone to

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:10.990
depression, something she can only get out
of - literally - with the help of her friends

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:14.280
[“Yeah, the men saved her” “ugh”].

00:09:14.280 --> 00:09:18.640
In the end, Big Mouth tells us that the best
way to deal with shame is communication. [“I

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:22.940
do need help. I wanna talk to that therapist.]
While having a free-for-all at a middle school

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:27.220
sleepover isn’t exactly the most practical
solution, being willing to talk about the

00:09:27.220 --> 00:09:32.010
way we feel keeps us from feeling alone. After
all, whether you’re an awkward middle schooler

00:09:32.010 --> 00:09:35.339
in the throes of puberty or a famously dead
jazz phenomenon, life... is a f*cked up mess.

00:09:35.339 --> 00:09:42.459
[“Life is a f*cked up mess. Life is a f*cked
up mess.”]. We’d might as well laugh about

00:09:42.459 --> 00:09:43.459
it.

00:09:43.459 --> 00:09:48.500
So yeah, I’m gonna give that pitch a solid
B. That’s partly because shame is admittedly

00:09:48.500 --> 00:09:53.110
the low-hanging fruit here. Sure, we can cite
studies that confirm our basic understanding

00:09:53.110 --> 00:09:57.740
of shame all day, but it’s kind of one note.
Maybe the Shame Wizard could’ve broached

00:09:57.740 --> 00:10:01.850
the idea that shame has some benefits for
your psyche and society at large, an idea

00:10:01.850 --> 00:10:05.829
that’s been argued by psychologists and
philosophers alike. The kids sort of touch

00:10:05.829 --> 00:10:10.949
on that at the close of season 2 [“I wanna
be a good person too, I do” “Oh, you do,

00:10:10.949 --> 00:10:14.199
you see, we’re not so different, are we?”]
but they don’t really explore it in any

00:10:14.199 --> 00:10:18.420
meaningful way, so we don’t have enough
detail for a meaty discussion.

00:10:18.420 --> 00:10:22.711
Still, Big Mouth really kills its illustration
of the power of shame during puberty. And

00:10:22.711 --> 00:10:27.600
smart stuff aside, if you’re a Homo Sapien
who has or is currently going through puberty,

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:29.410
Big Mouth is really relatable.

